  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Roupp gave them five solid innings and the bullpen cleaned up. But seven hits and one run with runners in scoring position is just not enough to beat anyone. Colorado took the lead in the fourth and the Giants never reclaimed it, chasing the game from that moment forward. By the time Eldridge's solo shot arrived in the eighth, it was show business. The real problem landed in the third inning. Two runners on, two outs, and nothing to show for it. That's when this game got decided.",
    "what_this_means": "They left four runners on base and converted one RISP situation into a run. Roupp and the bullpen. Sanmartin, Brubaker, Harris, and Foley combining for 2.4 scoreless innings. Kept them in a position to steal this. The opportunity was there. The execution wasn't. This is what slipping teams do: they get handed moments and let them walk. Four RISP at-bats, one productive one, and a division deficit that grew by another game because the bats couldn't find the leverage when it mattered. Inconsistency isn't a pattern you recover from. It's the game telling you something.",
    "what_to_watch": "Michael Lorenzen takes the mound tonight with a 6.83 ERA. Webb's been dominant over his last four starts at 2.42. And the window is there if the offense shows teeth early.",
